# Caldera Pump — sculpting and rigging brief

You are sculpting and rigging the Caldera Pump, the Holdfast's walking-beam engine — a
broad brass frame carrying a great iron rocking beam pivoted at its center, one end
weighted by a counterweight and the other driving a piston rod into the ground, with a long
intake snorkel reaching out to the deep water beside it and a flywheel turning on the frame
— as a 3D voxel model with a rig a game can pose at runtime. Build it with `voxel-anim`
(painting opaque cells one operation at a time) as a rigged model that reads unmistakably as
this beam engine, ticks over slowly on its own, and see-saws its beam hard when it is
pumping. There is no target model to copy.

This test measures creativity and craft, not instruction-following. The brief fixes what
the Pump is and how it must move; it deliberately does not hand you a parts list, joint
placements, or pose angles — working out the pieces a walking-beam engine whose beam rocks
needs, where they attach, and how they articulate is the test. Invent the rig.

## The volume and coordinate system

- The volume is **{{voxel.width}} wide (x) × {{voxel.height}} tall (y) × {{voxel.depth}} deep (z)**, in opaque voxels. It
  starts empty.
- x runs across the Pump, `0`–`{{voxel.maxX}}`. y runs up, `0` (bottom, the ground) to `{{voxel.maxY}}`
  (top). z runs front-to-back, `0`–`{{voxel.maxZ}}`.
- Forward is +z: the Pump's front face looks toward `z = {{voxel.maxZ}}` (the front). Up is +y.
- The Pump is a **static engine founded on the ground**: its broad frame sits flat on the
  ground at `y = 0` and does not move. The beam swings in a vertical plane; the **intake
  snorkel reaches out to one side** toward the water, so the model is deliberately **not
  fully mirror-symmetric** — that reaching snorkel is intended.
- The Pump is deliberately **broad and low-slung** — a wide frame carrying the beam across
  it, not a tall or narrow one. It reads as brass-and-iron machinery; nothing about it should
  read as a creature or as a building.
- Each part is sculpted separately with `voxel-anim --part <name>`, in this same volume's
  coordinates, positioned where the part sits on the assembled Pump (the beam already
  pivoted on the frame, the counterweight already at one end, the piston rod already at the
  other, the snorkel already reaching out to the side).

## Palette

Use only these opaque colors:

| Role | Hex |
| --- | --- |
| Brass — primary plating | `#c8a24a` |
| Brass dark — shadow, underside | `#8a6d2e` |
| Iron — mechanisms, pipework, recesses | `#3a3836` |
| Accent fitting — verdigris (the accent region) | `#2f7d72` |

Do **not** use steel `#b8bcc2`, gold `#ffce54`, or white `#dfeaea` anywhere on the model.
Those are the colors a game paints onto Holdfast accent fittings at run time (see *The
accent region*); baking any of them into the model corrupts the recolor the build runs on
every accent voxel.

## What the Pump is (and what is yours to invent)

Fixed — the Pump must read unmistakably as all of these:

- A **broad brass frame** founded flat on the ground, carrying the beam across it. Its
  shadowed seams and undersides are brass dark.
- A **great iron rocking beam** pivoted at its center on the frame, swinging in a vertical
  plane — the Pump's defining feature. It is a heavy see-sawing arm, not a small lever.
- A **counterweight** at one end of the beam and a **piston rod** at the other end that
  drives down into the ground, so the beam rocks the weight up as the rod drives down and
  back (see the animations).
- A **long intake snorkel** running from the frame out to one side, reaching toward the deep
  water the Pump drinks from — a pipe arm clearly reaching off the frame.
- A **flywheel** turning on the frame — a wheel that spins as the engine runs.

Everything else is yours to invent — the exact silhouette and proportions of the frame and
beam, how the counterweight and piston rod are shaped, how the snorkel reaches out, how the
flywheel is mounted, and how you break the Pump into rig parts. Nothing here prescribes a
shape; the test rewards a bold, characterful design that is unmistakably the Pump and
animates convincingly.

The Pump is a **structure of the Holdfast — brass-and-iron machinery, not a creature**. The
tell is the great rocking BEAM, the turning FLYWHEEL, and the reaching SNORKEL: a static
walking-beam engine founded on the ground, with nothing about it reading as a creature.

## The accent region — a hard contract

A game recolors this model at run time by finding every voxel of one reserved color and
repainting it. The fluid structures do not upgrade, so the Pump's accent is **always**
painted to brass dark — but the build runs that recolor **unconditionally** on load, so the
region must still be authored exactly right. That reserved color is the **verdigris accent**
`#2f7d72`, and it is what makes the contract work:

- Sculpt the **counterweight at the beam's end** and the **rim of the flywheel** — and only
  those — in `#2f7d72`.
- Use `#2f7d72` **nowhere else** on the model. Not a stray voxel, not a highlight, not a
  seam. Every voxel of it is treated as accent fitting.
- Each accent element must be a **clean, unbroken run** — the counterweight a solid mass, the
  flywheel rim a continuous ring — and clearly **visible from more than one angle**.

Everything else — the frame, the beam arm, the piston rod, the snorkel, the flywheel hub,
the recesses — keeps its own color and is never repainted. Get this wrong and the Pump
recolors wrong on load.

## The required animations — the fixed contract

`rig.json` is pre-seeded with two required animation declarations by name (you author the
motion). Author each with `voxel-anim define-animation` then `add-keyframe`, choosing the
period and each key's `--interp` (`constant`/`linear`/`bezier` or
`ease-in`/`ease-out`/`ease-in-out`, with optional `--in-handle`/`--out-handle`) so the
motion reads right — a slow tick-over at idle and a heavy, weighty stroke when pumping.

- **`idle`** — ticking over, not delivering (a self-playing idle, `loop = true`,
  `auto_play = true`). The flywheel turns slowly and the beam sits **nearly level, barely
  rocking**, so the Pump reads as an engine on standby, not working. It cycles on its own
  with no caller. Only the flywheel and the slight beam motion play; the frame and snorkel
  stay fixed. It loops seamlessly.
- **`pump`** — working (a game-triggered playable, `loop = true`). The beam **rocks through
  its full stroke, see-sawing hard** — the counterweight rising and falling, the piston rod
  driving down and up with real weight, and the flywheel spinning fast. This plays only while
  the Pump is delivering water, so it must read **unmistakably as working, plainly distinct
  from the slow idle**. The frame stays planted.

You may add your own extra parts, joints, or animations on top of this (for example a
trembling of the snorkel), but you must not drop or contradict the required `idle` and
`pump` animations, by those names.

## Working the tool

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on your
`PATH`. Define your own parts with `define-part` (giving each a parent and a pivot), sculpt
each by selecting it with `--part <name>`, set pivots with `set-pivot`, place joints with
`define-joint`, and author the two animations' keyframes with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Run `voxel-anim --help` for the available operations
(setting and clearing single voxels, filling and stroking boxes, 3D lines, spheres, and a
mirror plane), the rig subcommands, and the animation subcommands, and `voxel-anim
<operation> --help` for each one's exact flags. Call `voxel-anim` once per operation; run
`voxel-anim render` and read `parts/<part>.png` (and the assembled `scene/*.png` previews)
between calls to judge each part against this brief — that the beam pivots on the frame's
center, the counterweight and piston rod hang at its ends, the snorkel reaches out to the
side, the flywheel sits on the frame, and the two animations read with the right contrast
between ticking over and working. Run `voxel-anim render` before you finish so it emits the
per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built from — an unrendered part scores as empty
(`voxel-anim render --component <part>` renders one part; `voxel-anim render --time <ms>
--animation <name>` renders the model posed at that instant to check the motion).
